00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.670
What's the actual problem
that we're trying to address?

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.690
Well, typically, what
we see quite frequently

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:21.760
is when users, or
companies, or teams

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.320
get started with the cloud--

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.290
oh, yes, I have access
to a cloud project.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.720
That's great.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.770
And let's follow a
couple of tutorials,

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.050
or let's try a
bunch of things out.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.330
And then I copy
paste some commands.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.830
And then I click
around a little bit

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:37.780
to set up some configuration.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.130
And eventually, I have my
POC environment running,

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.718
or I've tried a few things out.

00:01:43.718 --> 00:01:45.760
And I've got a bunch of
things that I've created,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.090
maybe some networking config,
maybe a couple of clusters here

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.840
and there, maybe some VPC stuff,
and then a couple of buckets,

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.420
and so on and so forth.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.410
And I mean, there's
a whole bunch here,

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.570
some IM permissions.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.970
But it's kind of manageable,
and maybe some of it's

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:01.760
not necessary.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.510
But if I have a few things lying
around, that's not terrible.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.670
And actually, GCP
does a nice job

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.840
of warning you about things
that haven't been used

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.699
and so on and so forth.

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.113
But let's imagine you decide
that you really like this.

00:02:13.113 --> 00:02:14.530
This cloud stuff
is great, and you

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.630
feel it's going to help
your company a lot.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.790
And then you get all
your development teams

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.110
or your friends to work on it.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.060
And then you add more resources.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.640
Suddenly you've got a
whole bunch of projects.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.480
So maybe you want to start
doing some stuff at the more org

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.320
level.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.750
I mean org as in
the GCP construct.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.555
So you don't have to set
up the same permissions

00:02:34.555 --> 00:02:37.180
or the same policies, these kind
of things, all over the place.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.270
So you've now got two places
where your config is already

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.535
living.

00:02:41.535 --> 00:02:42.910
And then I think
you kind of know

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.670
where this is going,
dot, dot, dot, dot.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.810
Suddenly you're all
in on the cloud.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.080
You've got tons and
tons of projects.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.870
You've got org level config
that's quite complicated.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:56.230
And you have all these 500
projects, each one of which

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.260
is set up, well, similarly,
but then slightly differently.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.673
And some of them are maybe in
compliance and some of them

00:03:01.673 --> 00:03:02.590
are out of compliance.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.490
And really, config
sprawl, I think most of us

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.560
have heard this term.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.970
We don't want to go there.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.410
If we have been there already,
we want to get out of it.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.840
And if we haven't been
there yet, good for you.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.517
But we're going to talk about
how we can try to prevent this.

00:03:19.517 --> 00:03:21.100
What's the answer,
at least what we're

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.517
going to be talking
about today is

00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:26.058
how we can manage this
complexity with relatively

00:03:26.058 --> 00:03:28.600
well-known patterns, thankfully,
so there's nothing too crazy

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.280
here.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.800
Using infrastructure
as code and automation

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.520
to handle both the definition
and the safe rollout

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.400
of configuration to
all the various places

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.337
that we need to have in GCP.

00:03:42.337 --> 00:03:44.170
And we're going to look
at a couple of tools

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.952
to do this, a code repo.

00:03:45.952 --> 00:03:47.410
We're going to look
at Cloud Build.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.368
And we're going to look
at deployment templates

00:03:49.368 --> 00:03:51.670
here to reflect
the configuration

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.600
that we want to have.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.150
So what are some of the
benefits of using infrastructure

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:56.730
as code?

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:02.710
Well, similar benefits to using
any type of configured code.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.590
You've got all the nice bits
about using code management.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.260
Things are versioned.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.380
You have things like
stable IDs through commit,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.880
so that you can always point
back at a previous version.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.380
You can have multiple
people working

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:18.470
on the same things,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.150
You have this idea that rather
than infrastructure being

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.250
in whatever state
it happens to be in,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.410
you actually have a
place that specifies

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.070
what you want the
infrastructure to look like.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:32.980
And that's useful for things
like drift detection or just

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.120
as an audit trail.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.500
But of course,
it's relevant also

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.450
for things like disaster
recovery scenarios

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.500
if you want to bring back
something to this day

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.870
that it was and so on.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.460
It's nice and
declarative, so rather

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.330
than hiding the intended state
in a bunch of G Cloud commands

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.313
or in a bunch of
imperative commands,

00:04:48.313 --> 00:04:49.480
you can actually look at it.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:50.900
And it's hopefully
human readable,

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.483
and it gives you a
sense of what it is

00:04:52.483 --> 00:04:53.830
that you were going to set up.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:55.413
I talked about
auditable a little bit.

00:04:55.413 --> 00:04:57.460
And then of course,
it's modular if you

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:01.110
have the right type of conflict
as code or infrastructure

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:04.180
as code tooling or
language that you're using.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.460
You don't just have to have
one enormous flat file that

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.430
is copied or duplicated all over
the place, some parts of it.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.430
You have the ability to do
things like inherit sections

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.310
so that you can have a
shared definition for maybe

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.130
some networking
config that you use

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.600
in a whole bunch of
different places.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.090
So we talked about code.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.090
We talked about some of
the benefits of managing

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.682
this configuration as code.

00:05:29.682 --> 00:05:31.140
And what we've
learned when we talk

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.760
about source code
over the years is

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.440
that there are certain practices
that are really useful in order

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.570
to make sure that
we don't break stuff

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.550
when we build applications.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.250
And if we're going to
think through what's CI/CD

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.030
or what deployment might
look like for infrastructure

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:50.640
as code, we can use exactly
the same processes by analogy

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.780
to think through
what that might mean.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.890
So most of us have probably
heard of continuous integration

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:58.500
for application code,
the idea that whenever

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.770
I submit or propose a
change to my source code,

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.350
I should run a whole bunch
of tests and verifications

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.150
to make sure that
this change actually

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.570
is going to be good and not
just eyeball it and think

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:09.670
like, oh, it looks fine.

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:10.880
Let's try it out.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:12.630
And of course, we can
do something similar

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.410
for infrastructure as code.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.400
I'm proposing a change to my
infrastructure definitions,

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.830
maybe a change to a firewall
config or something like that.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:23.700
And automation can give me
a lot of information about

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.400
whether that change is safe,
whether that change is good.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.440
At the very simplest
level, automation

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.440
can tell me whether I actually
have made a syntax error.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.060
And the new configures code
is broken or is not broken.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.370
And so we'll talk a little
bit about how we can--

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.220
what kinds of information
we can feed back

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.087
to whoever is working with
the infrastructure as code

00:06:44.087 --> 00:06:46.170
in order to make sure that
we have good confidence

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:50.770
that the code change we're
proposing actually works.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.370
And then if we, again, we follow
that analogy a step further,

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.210
yes, we've assured ourselves
that a code change or a config

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.347
change that we're
going to make is safe.

00:06:59.347 --> 00:07:00.930
But now instead of
just saying, great,

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:03.970
let's just apply it everywhere
and see what happens,

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:06.150
what we've learned with
application releases

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.790
is that it makes sense to
have a gradual progression,

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:12.300
some process where we maybe
run some integration tests,

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.290
or we have a
staging environment,

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:16.020
or we maybe even
do advanced things,

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.670
like a blue green deployment.

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.650
There's lots of
practices that we've

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:23.580
discovered that make the
rollout of a new config

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.613
or a new application
change safer.

00:07:26.613 --> 00:07:29.030
And of course, we can do the
same thing for infrastructure

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:30.360
as code as well.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:34.860
We can think through how we can
safely roll out these config

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.800
changes progressively to the
places where they need to go,

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.230
as opposed to saying, oh,
I've approved the change.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.560
Now somebody gets their
laptop, clones the repo,

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:48.270
and goes apply or does that in
Cloud Shell or somewhere else.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.830
And as I hinted at when I talked
about the progressive rollout,

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.330
one of the dimensions that come
into this is that of course,

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:58.320
we have multiple
environments in most cases.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.260
You don't just have one
set of config for one GCP

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.850
project or one environment.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:07.230
It's very common that as we
think about our infrastructure

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:11.700
landscape, we have this
notion of logical differences.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.220
We have the dev environment,
or the test environment,

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:18.610
or the staging environment for
our teams or our applications.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.850
And we also have
inevitably differences

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.050
across those environments.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:25.260
And so what we want
to do is, A, we

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:27.810
want to be able to
reflect that difference

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.570
in our infrastructure as
code, so we can easily

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.880
see what the state of our
dev, and test, and staging

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.960
environments should be.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:37.799
And we also want to have a
rollout process that takes

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:40.630
these changes through the
various environments so that we

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:41.130
don't--

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.600
I mean, we can if we want to.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.850
But it's often safer rather
than applying the changes

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.335
to everything at once.

00:08:46.335 --> 00:08:47.460
Let's take an example case.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.472
If you're going to
change a firewall rule,

00:08:49.472 --> 00:08:51.930
it's kind of useful to change
it in the staging environment

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:55.590
first to see if your application
still works before you decide

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:57.060
to apply that same
kind of change

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.820
to your production environment,
where if you get it wrong,

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.820
you might be breaking your
users who suddenly can't access

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.650
the application anymore.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:06.732
So there's a longer--

00:09:06.732 --> 00:09:08.190
let me just pause
at this-- there's

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.010
a longer discussion
about how environments

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.090
map to different GCP projects.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:15.960
And I think the short answer is
that there is no right answer,

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.690
but we are very happy to talk
about different patterns there.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.815
Suffice it to say that from
the perspective of what

00:09:20.815 --> 00:09:23.400
we're going to talk about
today, it doesn't really matter.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.260
Your dev, and test, and
staging environments

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.052
could all be resources
in the same project,

00:09:27.052 --> 00:09:30.160
or they could be spread
across multiple projects.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:34.950
So having taken these
principles about CI and CD,

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:37.980
let's now look at how we
can actually technically use

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:41.603
tools that are available to
implement this kind of flow.

00:09:41.603 --> 00:09:43.020
So what we're going
to have, we're

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.410
going to have a code repo
that has infrastructure

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:50.160
as code definitions for
multiple environments.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:53.900
We're going to submit or propose
a change to this definition.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:55.620
Or rather, we're going
to work as I just

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.200
do the handwaving slide bit.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.570
We're going to have CI,
an automation process,

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:04.380
providing feedback
on that pull request

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.045
to tell us about whether it's
a good pull request or not.

00:10:07.045 --> 00:10:08.420
And we'll talk
about some tooling

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.400
we can use as part
of that process

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.790
to give us more
useful information.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.540
Then we're going to
have a review process.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.340
The nice thing about
using a code repo

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.160
is that we kind of get that for
free through the pull request

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:20.200
model.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.430
And there's actually
some nice-- depending

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.380
on which repository
service you're

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:26.810
using, whether it's GitHub,
or GitLab, or Bitbucket,

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:27.740
or whatever.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:28.830
Did I say GitHab?

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:30.530
GitHub.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:33.335
You have nice options about
adding access control,

00:10:33.335 --> 00:10:35.960
and you can require that certain
people are the only people who

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:38.177
can do reviews and so on.

00:10:38.177 --> 00:10:40.010
Somebody is going to
review this code change

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:43.730
after we've got the feedback for
things like policy compliance.

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:46.920
Then we're going to decide maybe
or maybe not it's good to go.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.753
And then, of course,
once it's done,

00:10:48.753 --> 00:10:50.420
we are going to take
this config change,

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:51.795
and we're actually
going to apply

00:10:51.795 --> 00:10:54.080
it progressively to the
various environments

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.840
that it has to go to.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.590
So what are some of
the design principles,

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.390
if you like, in terms
of the automation

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:02.910
that we're going to
be looking at here?

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:04.410
We're going to be
using some release

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:07.260
pipelines to do the automation.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.067
So that's not going to be
somebody following a Wiki page.

00:11:11.067 --> 00:11:12.900
That's actually going
to be fully automated.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.850
And again, we're
going to see that we

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:16.740
can store this
automation in the repo,

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.260
as well as config as code.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.510
We're going to have
a sort of equivalent

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.522
to integration
testing or to testing.

00:11:24.522 --> 00:11:25.980
Obviously, for
application code, we

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.650
understand what
integration testing means.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:32.070
But there are analogies for
infrastructure as code as well.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.770
The pull requests give
us a manual judgment,

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.330
and of course,
given that we have

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.700
the last version of the
config in our repository,

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:41.460
because we're just changing
it in a version store,

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:44.280
we also have a nice vector
to do things like rollbacks.

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.350
So basically, I guess
the long story short

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:49.620
is that all the good design
principles about CI and CD

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:52.770
that we apply for
applications, we

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:57.190
are also able to apply for
infrastructure as code here.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:02.490
So to get a little bit more
detailed, this all sounds,

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.805
I think, hopefully very
nice and compelling.

00:12:04.805 --> 00:12:06.180
But now you actually
have to make

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.700
some concrete decisions
about, OK, I've

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:10.468
got this batch of
infrastructure as code.

00:12:10.468 --> 00:12:11.760
I've got multiple environments.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.960
I'm going to roll them out.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:17.730
How do I reflect that
in my repository?

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:20.640
And again, while
caveating it by saying

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:24.180
that there is no perfect
answer for all situations, what

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:26.850
we're going to present here
is a templated pattern.

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.850
Or one particular suggestion
that you can use and you can

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.430
think about using
in your setup--

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:34.680
and again, this is not
the only way to do things,

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:37.110
and I'm very happy to
discuss variations depending

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:38.920
on your use cases afterwards.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.470
But we think this is a
really good place to start.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:45.960
So what we start with is we
have a master branch that will

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:47.340
include in different folders--

00:12:50.603 --> 00:12:52.020
yes, we start with
a master branch

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:53.790
that will include
in different folders

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:56.508
the configuration
that we want to have

00:12:56.508 --> 00:12:58.050
in each of the
different environments

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.060
that we're rolling out to.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.790
Now this means that this
is what we want to have.

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:05.640
This doesn't necessarily
reflect what is actually running

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.530
in each of those environments.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:10.020
But it tells us what
the kind of like this

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.060
is what we want to move
towards, and especially

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.890
as we make changes
to it, so we might

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.710
open a pull request against
this branch, for instance.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:20.970
To change the information
in the prod folder,

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:23.130
that means that we are
thinking about making

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:24.510
a change to the prod config.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.960
It doesn't mean that the
prod config has changed yet.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.750
One of the things that we've
learned around Kubernetes--

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:32.550
this is very popular,
but in fact, this

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.590
can be done with
any kind of system,

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:38.040
is the idea that we have
GitOps or environments as code

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.630
as I like to call it.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:41.750
It's very useful
not just to have

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:45.060
what we're talking about here,
which is that you have folders

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.340
that represent
the desired state,

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.620
but that you
actually reflect also

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.290
inversion control,
what the actual state

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.100
of your environment is.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:55.860
So as we start doing
the deployment,

00:13:55.860 --> 00:14:00.330
as we start applying this config
across our various resources

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:05.350
in GCP, we want to have
that actual state reflected

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:06.677
in our repo as well.

00:14:06.677 --> 00:14:08.260
Because that gives
us a history of not

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:10.135
just of what we wanted
to happen, but of what

00:14:10.135 --> 00:14:11.480
actually happened.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:17.140
So what you will see is
that the state of master

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:20.300
is intended to reflect the
state of the dev environment.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:20.800
Right?

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.022
So what is in master when
something is in master,

00:14:24.022 --> 00:14:26.230
we think that that is what
the dev environment should

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:26.930
look like.

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:28.413
And so the automation
is set up--

00:14:28.413 --> 00:14:30.580
you'll see that in the demo
that as we merge changes

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:33.640
into master, the
job is or the intent

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:35.890
is that that should mean
make the dev environment

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:37.575
look like this.

00:14:37.575 --> 00:14:38.950
The next stage in
the progression

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:41.290
is once we're happy
with the dev environment

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:45.220
and we want to move
forward, we will

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:47.950
take the staging environment,
the information that's

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:51.023
in the staging branch, and we
will apply that to the staging

00:14:51.023 --> 00:14:53.440
environment when it's merged
to a particular branch called

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.510
staging.

00:14:55.510 --> 00:14:58.030
And then when we are happy
with the outcome of that,

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:59.350
we think everything is OK.

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:00.555
Staging actually looks OK.

00:15:00.555 --> 00:15:01.680
The apps are still running.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:04.780
We want to go forward and affect
our production environment.

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:07.030
We will take the changes,
and we will apply them also

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:09.070
to the prod branch.

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:12.010
And that will show us
what is actually in prod.

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:16.462
So if you look at the
prod branch in your repo,

00:15:16.462 --> 00:15:18.170
that will tell you
what actually happened

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:20.140
or what the state of the
production environment

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:20.973
really is right now.

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:27.090
It's somewhat abstract.

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:29.090
And it will become, I
think, much more real when

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:30.700
you see the demo in a second.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:32.990
And so for that, let me
hand over to the magic

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:34.550
that Morgante is
about to show us.

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.650
MORGANTE PELL: Thanks, Andrew.

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:40.940
Sorry, skipped ahead there.

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:43.012
So for my first demo
I want to focus on

00:15:43.012 --> 00:15:44.470
is actually the
shared foundations.

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.850
That's something we were talking
about that's not necessarily

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:47.690
project specific.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:51.410
This is things like
networking, billing, IAM,

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:53.180
security controls,
all that stuff

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:54.908
that is shared across
the organization.

00:15:54.908 --> 00:15:56.450
Often, you have a
central team that's

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:57.470
running this sort of thing.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:59.220
And really, it's very
important because it

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.500
affects so many different
parts of the organization.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:02.990
And so many different
applications

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:04.708
can depend on a shared
VPC, for example.

00:16:04.708 --> 00:16:06.500
So we're going to show
how you can actually

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.090
do that through
infrastructure as code

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:10.010
and have the same
pattern of rolling it out

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:11.930
across multiple environments.

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:16.730
Specifically, using Terraform,
which is a open source tool

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:18.530
developed by our
partner HashiCorp.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.450
It has support from
multiple clouds,

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:21.713
but great support for Google.

00:16:21.713 --> 00:16:23.630
And in fact, we actually
have a full time team

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:25.520
of Google engineers
who work to make sure

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.595
that there's good support
for Terraform on GCP.

00:16:27.595 --> 00:16:29.720
All the same patterns that
we're talking about here

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:32.570
can be used with more or
less any infrastructure

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:33.170
as code tool.

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:35.290
You can use them with Google
Cloud Deployment Manager.

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:36.590
You can use it with Terraform.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:37.757
You can use it with Ansible.

00:16:37.757 --> 00:16:40.117
You can use it with whatever
tool you're using to.

00:16:40.117 --> 00:16:42.200
But today, we're going to
be looking at Terraform.

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:43.270
And one of the big things
that it actually has

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:45.145
is different plugins
and different providers.

00:16:45.145 --> 00:16:47.960
So the Google provider
is maintained primarily

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:49.640
by Google engineers.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.080
So just to go ahead to
the demo, there we go.

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:53.580
Cool.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:56.400
So here I have my
deployment repo.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:57.650
So this is just a simple repo.

00:16:57.650 --> 00:17:01.010
It's for my little sample
organization that I have.

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:03.273
And one of the big things
that we have as a principle

00:17:03.273 --> 00:17:04.940
is that you actually
want to separate it

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:07.220
out so that you don't
have all of your code

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:10.160
in a single folder for all
of your environments or even

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.540
for a single environment,
all the different purposes.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:14.260
You want to separate that
out by different purposes

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:15.589
and particularly by
different teams that

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:16.589
might be maintaining it.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:18.422
So often a pattern we'll
see is that there's

00:17:18.422 --> 00:17:20.990
a networking team, for example,
that's maintaining networking

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:23.442
config comprised of both
automation engineers,

00:17:23.442 --> 00:17:24.859
as well as any
traditional network

00:17:24.859 --> 00:17:28.020
engineers that might be involved
in looking at that as well.

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:30.533
So within this
networking folder,

00:17:30.533 --> 00:17:33.200
like Andrew was talking about, I
have my different environments.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:36.080
In this demo, I'm just doing
a very simple dev and prod.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.180
So we can have simplicity
there, where we have

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:39.540
each of the Terraform files.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.540
One of the things you
notice here is we actually

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:43.315
also have some
files on the root.

00:17:43.315 --> 00:17:44.690
This is for files
that are shared

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:45.898
across multiple environments.

00:17:45.898 --> 00:17:48.213
So I don't have to copy and
paste the exact same file

00:17:48.213 --> 00:17:49.130
into each environment.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.650
I can actually have a shared
file and just sim link it

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:53.630
into each directory where
it needs to be used.

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:56.040
And Terraform automatically
can pick those up.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:57.470
Deployment Manager can as well.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:17:59.510
So you can very easily share
code across these environments

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:01.510
if you need to if you
want to have the same file

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.180
without copying and pasting.

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.670
So let's jump into
dev environment here.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:08.747
So here are a couple things
that matter in this folder.

00:18:08.747 --> 00:18:10.580
Each of my environments,
I want to make sure

00:18:10.580 --> 00:18:11.930
that I have a backend config.

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:14.270
This is used for storing the
Terraform state so that I'm

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:15.950
making sure that
every change I make

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:18.457
is actually stored on
Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:18.457 --> 00:18:19.790
So I can roll back if I need to.

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:21.320
I have version history there.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:24.140
If I want to collaborate across
multiple different CI runs,

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:25.590
it actually has
locking in place,

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.798
so it can make sure that
two different CI runs aren't

00:18:27.798 --> 00:18:30.290
attempting to manipulate
state at the same time,

00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:35.510
as well as the ability to
revert back if necessary.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:37.130
And then I've got
the provider config.

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:39.920
I've got a couple of variables
for things like what my org

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:41.510
idea is, if I want
to be able to have

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:44.090
that be easily substitutable.

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:46.940
And then in my main.tf is where
I start actually configuring

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:50.000
what my dev networking
environment looks like.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.860
As you notice, most
of the actual code

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.990
that goes into these
individual environment folders

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:55.948
can actually module or
template reference this.

00:18:55.948 --> 00:18:57.907
Because in many cases,
you want to share a code

00:18:57.907 --> 00:18:59.400
across different environments.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:01.790
So you don't want to actually
have all of the config

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:05.570
and all of the complexity be
in the individual environments

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:06.320
with stakeholders.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:08.570
You just want to have the
configuration, the specifics

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:09.590
of that environment.

00:19:09.590 --> 00:19:11.295
So here, I'm using
this VPC module,

00:19:11.295 --> 00:19:12.920
which is one of our
open source modules

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.380
that we have for working
with Terraform on Google

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:18.880
Cloud, which is used for
setting up a shared VPC network.

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:20.790
So I specify I want
my project ideas.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:22.310
I specify which subnets I want.

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:23.930
So I want two subnets in here.

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:27.430
I specify any secondary ranges
I want for the subnets as well.

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:30.410
And then this is all, of
course, very specific to the dev

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:30.960
environment.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:33.127
So I'm not going use the
same IP space, for example,

00:19:33.127 --> 00:19:34.890
between both dev and
prod in most cases.

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:35.390
Right?

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:37.182
So this is configuration
that matters, just

00:19:37.182 --> 00:19:40.490
the dev environment, but I'm
able to use the same module

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:41.832
in both environments.

00:19:41.832 --> 00:19:44.290
Other parts of the configuration
might be even more shared,

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.160
where it's the case where you
have the exact same firewall

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:47.820
rules in both environments.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:50.390
So this is where this shared
firewall rules module comes in.

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:52.010
I'm able to reference
this module.

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:54.810
And just passing the dev
specific information,

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:56.950
like the network name
and the host project,

00:19:56.950 --> 00:19:58.453
it applies for my
dev environment

00:19:58.453 --> 00:19:59.870
into the shared
modules so I'm not

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:02.300
having to write the
same firewall config

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.760
for each environment I'm doing.

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:08.040
So that's just a relative
reference to the same repo.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.362
So it goes up to this
other modules directory,

00:20:10.362 --> 00:20:12.320
where I've actually kind
of shared bits of code

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:14.180
that I want to share across
different environments.

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:16.097
It's very easy to build
a module or a template

00:20:16.097 --> 00:20:17.820
in both Terraform and
Deployment Manager.

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:19.970
You just create a
file, create a folder.

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:21.620
You have a module instantly.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:23.120
There's no registration process.

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:24.745
There's nothing
special you have to do.

00:20:24.745 --> 00:20:26.390
Any folder with a
Terraform file in it

00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:27.870
automatically becomes a module.

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:31.670
So this is my module just for
shared firewall rules here.

00:20:31.670 --> 00:20:34.090
Very simple, just has
a couple of inputs

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:36.050
of which project I'm
hosting my network in

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:37.550
and the network
that's on that pro--

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:40.250
that's the name for that
network, which I also

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:41.540
use in the firewall name.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:42.950
And of course, I have my rules.

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:44.960
Right now, I only have
a single firewall rule,

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:46.250
which allows HTTPS.

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:47.750
But let's go ahead,
and modify that,

00:20:47.750 --> 00:20:49.950
and add an additional rule.

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:52.680
Let's say we realize we
actually have to at least sort

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:55.640
of redirect on HTTP because
people are going to our website

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:56.840
and getting frustrated.

00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:58.650
They couldn't access it.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:01.310
So we're going to just
add an allow HTTP rule.

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:03.690
Let's put it there.

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:06.170
And let's just use the same--

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:07.980
we can use the same target tag.

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:09.132
So this config looks good.

00:21:09.132 --> 00:21:11.090
And I can be really
dangerous here and actually

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:14.078
just commit it directly
into the master branch.

00:21:14.078 --> 00:21:16.620
Obviously, not what you'd want
to do in the real environment,

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:17.870
but we can start
to see and we'll

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:19.620
talk more about how
you can actually build

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:21.000
an overall pipeline for that.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:22.820
But we'll just say
Add New Firewall Rule.

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:27.570
All right.

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:33.040
And as soon as this is pushed
into the master branch,

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:35.900
Cloud Build picks it up and is
going to start to build that.

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:36.400
Right?

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:38.590
So here you see push
to the master branch.

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:40.670
Deploy networking to
the dev environment.

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:42.045
So we have a Cloud
Build pipeline

00:21:42.045 --> 00:21:44.550
that automatically captures
that, runs Terraform

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:46.217
init in the direct
in there to make sure

00:21:46.217 --> 00:21:48.133
that all the modules are
downloaded, make sure

00:21:48.133 --> 00:21:50.530
everything's in place, to
be able to run everything.

00:21:50.530 --> 00:21:52.210
You can see it pulling all
the Docker containers here

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:53.380
to run everything there.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:56.270
And then actually goes and
applies it to my environment.

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:59.530
So here, we see the Google
provider, the latest version

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:02.947
because I didn't have it pinned,
and then the null provider.

00:22:02.947 --> 00:22:05.030
And then we can actually
see what's being created.

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:07.630
So we can see the allow
HTTP firewall rule is

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:10.060
being created in my dev
environment on that project

00:22:10.060 --> 00:22:11.793
as I expected.

00:22:11.793 --> 00:22:13.960
And we notice we actually
have so many variables get

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:17.020
interpreted, so
dev VPC allow HTTP.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:18.610
So we had that as expected.

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:20.260
And everything is complete.

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:21.410
All set.

00:22:21.410 --> 00:22:24.370
So if I go over here,
go to my firewall rules,

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:29.320
I can see I now have the dev
VPC allow HTTPS and allow HTTP.

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:30.790
But this isn't on
prod yet, right?

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:31.930
Because we only did
it on dev, and we

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:33.550
want to make sure
everything's working great.

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:35.410
We want to make sure we
get a chance to test that.

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:36.827
Maybe we do some
security testing,

00:22:36.827 --> 00:22:39.040
make sure that we're not
somehow exposing application

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:40.600
vulnerabilities that way.

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:42.160
But it's only on dev.

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:43.660
Now let's say
everything looks good.

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:44.950
The firewall rule's great.

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:47.365
We want to promote the
same change to production.

00:22:47.365 --> 00:22:49.990
That's just the process of doing
a pull request from the master

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:52.370
branch to the production branch.

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:56.560
So I'm going to go
ahead and go to my root,

00:22:56.560 --> 00:22:57.810
and create a new pull request.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:04.040
And so the base I'm still
going to do on my repo.

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:07.637
And I want it to be actually
my production branch, right?

00:23:07.637 --> 00:23:09.470
Only change here was
that new firewall rule.

00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:10.850
Otherwise, they're up to date.

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:13.910
So we can say, yeah, I'm
going to do this as a release

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:14.940
candidate basically.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:15.440
Right?

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:17.940
So I'm going to say this is
what I want to move from the dev

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:19.377
branch into production.

00:23:19.377 --> 00:23:20.960
There's a pull request
here so there's

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:23.660
a chance for anyone who wants to
give feedback on it, they can.

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:26.720
They can come in and provide any
details they want, everything

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:27.380
there.

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.547
Maybe you have to have a
certain number of reviewers

00:23:29.547 --> 00:23:31.040
happen on any production push.

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:32.750
You could say that they have
to come into the pull request

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:34.570
and put the looks
good to me for them.

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:36.320
But you can actually
have all your reviews

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:38.000
and all of your
processes in place

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:39.272
just through the pull request.

00:23:39.272 --> 00:23:40.730
Because that's sort
of your ability

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:42.897
to have manual judgments
in place, as well as an ACI

00:23:42.897 --> 00:23:44.690
you want to run.

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.640
Says there's no conflict,
so I can go ahead and merge.

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:49.200
So I'm just in a merger.

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:54.480
And that'll kick off an
additional Cloud Build.

00:23:57.879 --> 00:23:58.780
Yep.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.380
So we push the
production branch.

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:02.850
So now we can deploy
networking to production.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:04.700
It's going to run along.

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:06.875
Do the same changes
there, and in a minute,

00:24:06.875 --> 00:24:09.000
it'll show up here with my
new firewall rule there.

00:24:12.458 --> 00:24:14.250
One of the other things
you can do actually

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:15.960
is also with the
folder structure

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:17.940
that I was talking
about, you can actually

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:20.200
have different owners
for different folders.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.905
So you can do something with--

00:24:22.905 --> 00:24:25.278
most Git systems support
some form of ownership.

00:24:25.278 --> 00:24:26.820
But you can basically
have an owner's

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.800
file in each of these
directories and similar

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:30.175
to what we did
with Google, where

00:24:30.175 --> 00:24:32.010
you can say the networking--

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:34.678
we want our network team
member to give an approval

00:24:34.678 --> 00:24:36.720
on any change that touches
this part of the code.

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:38.400
So you can put that
in the owner's file

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:39.572
in the networking folder.

00:24:39.572 --> 00:24:41.280
You could have a
different part for, say,

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:44.053
security changes that you
want to have a team there.

00:24:44.053 --> 00:24:45.720
So you can have a lot
of configurability

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:48.300
over who has to approve
different pull request types,

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:51.610
based on what infrastructure
is being affected.

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:52.110
Cool.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.380
All right, that's finished.

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:56.443
And there we go.

00:24:56.443 --> 00:24:58.110
We have the same
change applied to prod.

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:00.060
We had conference, and then we
had the exact same commit being

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:01.248
pushed into both branches.

00:25:01.248 --> 00:25:02.790
So we have full
confidence that we're

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:06.974
having the same changes being
promoted across the lifecycle.

00:25:06.974 --> 00:25:07.474
Cool.

00:25:07.474 --> 00:25:08.682
We can go back to the slides.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:12.760
Cool.

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:14.280
So I mentioned modules.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.710
They're a primary method
of code reuse in Terraform.

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:18.430
I showed the definition of this.

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:20.230
One of the really nice things
is you can load modules

00:25:20.230 --> 00:25:21.410
from many different sources.

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:23.320
So you can do it from
a local repository,

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:24.280
like I showed there.

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:25.863
You can pull from
the Terraform module

00:25:25.863 --> 00:25:29.290
registry, which is lots of best
practice modules out there.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:30.520
So you don't have to go out
and write your own module

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:31.978
every time you want
to do a network

00:25:31.978 --> 00:25:33.580
or do a firewall
rule, for example.

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.955
And you can also pull modules
straight from GitHub, too--

00:25:35.955 --> 00:25:37.510
or actually, any Git instance.

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:39.610
So if you have an
internal GitLab repo,

00:25:39.610 --> 00:25:40.950
you can easily pull from there.

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:42.492
You can easily share
modules in that.

00:25:42.492 --> 00:25:45.955
They're the best way to actually
do code reuse in Terraform.

00:25:45.955 --> 00:25:48.330
In fact, we've done something
called the Cloud Foundation

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:50.910
Toolkit, which is a set
of example and sample

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:53.850
modules for Terraform that
represent our best practices

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:56.070
and experience with
Enterprise customers.

00:25:56.070 --> 00:25:59.300
So these cover things like
creating your core VPCs,

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:01.140
creating projects
in a repeatable way,

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:04.050
managing IAM permissions,
creating GKE clusters

00:26:04.050 --> 00:26:06.278
that are secure and
deployable effectively.

00:26:06.278 --> 00:26:08.820
And we have these templates for
both Terraform and Deployment

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:11.430
Manager that you can download
totally open source, all

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:12.810
on GitHub.

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:15.100
And we actually, for Next,
made a nice website for it.

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:17.558
So you can go there, and I'll
have the links to everything.

00:26:17.558 --> 00:26:20.028
And it shows you how to use
it and some tutorials there.

00:26:20.028 --> 00:26:21.570
The benefit of this
is you actually--

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:23.940
is that you have to discover
all the best practices yourself

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:26.398
and every team that comes onto
GCP having to figure out how

00:26:26.398 --> 00:26:29.430
to create a project, you can
just take the modules that has

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:31.220
all the best practices
baked into them,

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:33.220
and you can focus on the
parts that are specific

00:26:33.220 --> 00:26:35.260
to your application use
case and what's needed,

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:36.393
particularly for your--

00:26:36.393 --> 00:26:38.060
what's different about
your environment,

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:41.070
instead of doing all the
baseline stuff yourself.

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:43.230
We also have some sample
Cloud Build pipelines.

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.070
We'll integrate that
into there as well.

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:46.410
And the other thing
we're trying to do

00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:47.785
is actually have
secured defaults

00:26:47.785 --> 00:26:48.900
for all of these modules.

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:51.005
Because of the way
that GCP is built,

00:26:51.005 --> 00:26:52.380
we actually want
that really easy

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:54.297
getting started experience
that Andrew showed,

00:26:54.297 --> 00:26:56.070
that you can go on,
start your free trial,

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:58.698
start planning some services,
do a POC very easily.

00:26:58.698 --> 00:27:00.240
And that's really
powerful because we

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:03.450
can have that power of the cloud
to experiment and play freely.

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:06.125
But once you start going
to the state where you're

00:27:06.125 --> 00:27:07.500
in an Enterprise
use case, you're

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.320
doing hundreds of applications,
you're moving entire workloads

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:13.470
over, you want a little bit more
consistency and repeatability.

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.000
And you want more
secure defaults.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:16.430
So that's what we baked
into these modules.

00:27:16.430 --> 00:27:18.750
So these are things like
making sure that you actually

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:21.690
don't have internet egress
by default on any project you

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:24.950
create, making sure that you
have GKE clusters by default

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:27.630
or have private IPs, as
opposed to public IPs.

00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:29.130
So it's a lot of
Enterprise defaults

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:31.338
that we bake into these
modules as well, just focused

00:27:31.338 --> 00:27:33.120
on sort of the
Enterprise use case

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:36.554
where it's the hobbyist
or PSC use case.

00:27:36.554 --> 00:27:38.770
Cool.

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:40.740
So I thought I said that
I was doing something

00:27:40.740 --> 00:27:41.890
really dangerous earlier.

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:44.190
And in fact, I was when
I committed directly

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:46.470
to production, right?

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:48.595
I able to push that change
directly to development.

00:27:48.595 --> 00:27:50.095
There were still
pull requests where

00:27:50.095 --> 00:27:51.960
I was going from
development to production.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:55.020
But ideally, no changes are
made to infrastructure and all

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:56.700
without some CI feedback.

00:27:56.700 --> 00:27:57.840
When we were talking about
the earlier pipeline,

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:00.000
we were talking about
having CI, so feedback that

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.590
allows you to do
some sort of testing

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:04.020
and sanity testing on your
code before it actually

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:05.370
goes into production.

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:09.180
So that's where having PCI
and Cloud Build comes in

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:11.880
and the ability to make sure
that human error's accounted

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:12.450
for.

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:16.165
We found that the most
common cases of insecurity

00:28:16.165 --> 00:28:17.790
in the cloud is
usually because there's

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:20.470
some human misconfiguration,
some mistake or just

00:28:20.470 --> 00:28:22.140
simple misunderstanding
of how the--

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:24.190
or even a typo of how
the configure's set up.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:27.300
It's often easier to
have miscommunications.

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:29.410
Especially when
everything's in code,

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:31.140
the machine is
exactly translating

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:31.860
what you have in the code.

00:28:31.860 --> 00:28:33.860
So you want to make sure
that your code is right

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:35.800
before you actually apply it.

00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:38.520
That's why it's easy--
and then we also

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:40.650
realize that actually can
slow down cloud adoption.

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:41.880
Especially for
Enterprise customers,

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:44.100
where you if you're worried
that developers could easily

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:45.517
make a mistake,
and do what I did,

00:28:45.517 --> 00:28:47.990
and just go commit
directly into master

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:50.220
and have that deploy into
GCP environment, that

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:52.470
can make security administrators
a little bit worried.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:53.340
You want to make
sure that you have

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:54.850
the right guardrails in place.

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:57.183
You want to make sure that
you have everything you need,

00:28:57.183 --> 00:29:00.630
which is why we are introducing
the idea of the Terraform

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:03.420
Vaildator, which can actually
look at your Terraform config,

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:06.530
run policy checks
against it, and determine

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:09.030
whether that Terraform config
violates any of these policies

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:10.630
before any changes are made.

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:12.630
So this allows you to
have confidence in changes

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:14.213
before they're
deployed to production.

00:29:14.213 --> 00:29:16.160
Before any of your GCP
resources are updated,

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:17.700
it can actually
look at that config

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:18.990
and give you full
confidence that things

00:29:18.990 --> 00:29:21.420
like no external IPs are being
exposed, that your firewall

00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:24.180
rules are not too broad,
that whatever policies matter

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:26.310
to your organization, you
can have them embedded

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:28.650
into your repo, embedded
into your CI pipeline,

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:31.650
that you can have the
full flexibility there.

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:35.220
We released this very
recently-- this V0.1,

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:37.657
I would say alpha
software at this point.

00:29:37.657 --> 00:29:38.490
But it's very early.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:40.823
But the idea is also, we're
trying to build a community.

00:29:40.823 --> 00:29:43.340
It's all open source, so
everyone can collaborate on it.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:46.240
It's a CI binary
you can download.

00:29:46.240 --> 00:29:47.420
You can work with it.

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:49.657
And the actual policy
library is also open source.

00:29:49.657 --> 00:29:51.990
So we've started with a couple
of handful of constraints

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:53.370
that we think are
best practices, things

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:55.218
like not having external
IPs, but we want

00:29:55.218 --> 00:29:56.510
to have more of them over time.

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:58.530
And we encourage everybody
to contribute to that

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:01.200
and actually have a full
community driven ecosystem

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:03.490
of policies that everyone
can contribute, share,

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:05.942
and sort of have policy
configuration as code

00:30:05.942 --> 00:30:07.900
the same way that we're
talking about Terraform

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:09.555
modules for your actual code.

00:30:09.555 --> 00:30:10.930
You can have the
same reusability

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:14.490
there across the board with
the Terraform Validator.

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.500
How this actually
works in practice

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:19.020
is as an administrator, you
go to the Terraform Validator

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:21.540
repo, and you say--
oh, OK, here's

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:23.760
the set of available policies,
available constraints.

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:26.260
I'm going to pick these three
constraints that matter to me.

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:30.540
No external IPs, no sharing IAM
resources outside of my domain,

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:33.840
and every project
that gets created

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:36.030
must have a label that's
the cost center for it.

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:37.493
These are totally flexible.

00:30:37.493 --> 00:30:39.660
Administrators can define
which ones matter to them.

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:41.770
But you can take it, put
it into your own repo.

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:43.800
So you take the public
repo, fork it, pull it

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:46.320
into your private repo,
and enable the policies

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:47.280
that matter to you.

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:49.710
And then the administrator
says, here's our policy repo.

00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:52.710
Developers, anytime you're
going to deploying to GCP,

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:54.210
it has to pass this repo.

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:55.990
If it doesn't, you're
not getting to GCP.

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:58.065
Then as a developer,
I have the flexibility

00:30:58.065 --> 00:31:00.120
to actually start
working on code locally

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:01.800
and know if it's
going break policy

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:03.675
before I actually even
submit it to everyone.

00:31:03.675 --> 00:31:05.790
So I kind of have a
very fast feedback cycle

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:08.415
where I know I'm breaking policy
before I even submit the code.

00:31:08.415 --> 00:31:08.915
Right?

00:31:08.915 --> 00:31:11.750
So I, as a developer, locally
write some Terraform code.

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:13.500
I can run the Terraform
Validator locally,

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:14.850
get immediate feedback there.

00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:17.562
And then once I submit it
up into a Git repo as a pull

00:31:17.562 --> 00:31:19.020
request, I also
get feedback there.

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:20.690
So I can make sure
it's enforced.

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:22.180
Assuming the CI
check looks good,

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:24.270
then I can actually finally
merge into production

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:25.740
or merge into
development branches,

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:29.314
and I have the full
visibility there.

00:31:29.314 --> 00:31:29.993
Cool.

00:31:29.993 --> 00:31:31.410
The other part of
this is actually

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:33.450
that you take those same checks,
the checks that we were talking

00:31:33.450 --> 00:31:35.700
about where you have the
pre-deployment checks as part

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:37.740
of the Terraform, and
those same policy library

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:40.857
can be used with Forseti, which
is our open source security

00:31:40.857 --> 00:31:43.440
monitoring product, which allows
you to do ongoing monitoring.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:46.680
So in case some developer
somehow got console access,

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:48.998
or there's some legacy
infrastructure that

00:31:48.998 --> 00:31:51.540
was in place before you had the
Terraform Validator in place,

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:52.165
you have Forseti.

00:31:52.165 --> 00:31:54.123
It does ongoing monitoring
against the same set

00:31:54.123 --> 00:31:56.070
of policies and just
detection if there's

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:57.150
any violations there.

00:31:57.150 --> 00:32:00.000
And we'll send you alerts, put
them into the Cloud Security

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:03.100
Command Center, or send you
email alerts that you're

00:32:03.100 --> 00:32:04.470
getting Slack notification.

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:07.070
Make sure that if there's a
violation, you know about it.

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:09.162
And you can actually
remediate it if necessary.

00:32:09.162 --> 00:32:10.620
I mean, you can go
in and find out.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:13.440
But so you have both sides,
the same policy library

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:14.240
use in both places.

00:32:14.240 --> 00:32:15.990
So you're both doing
pre-deployment checks

00:32:15.990 --> 00:32:18.450
and ongoing monitoring to make
sure policies are in place

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:20.980
before infrastructure is
created and as infrastructure

00:32:20.980 --> 00:32:22.230
matures and changes over time.

00:32:24.770 --> 00:32:27.740
How the policies actually
look like in practice,

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:30.080
everything is very declarative
in nature, very similar

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:32.940
to Kubernetes-style APIs.

00:32:32.940 --> 00:32:34.820
So if anyone's worked
with Kubernetes,

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:37.110
this looks very similar
to a Kubernetes manifest.

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:40.280
So you have in here this is
for an IAM policy member,

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:42.530
so you can say what
constraint I'm using.

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:45.350
So this is the IAM allowed
policy member domain

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:46.130
constraint.

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:48.683
So it's just specifying
where are members

00:32:48.683 --> 00:32:50.350
of my organization
allowed to come from,

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:52.430
so what domains are they
allowed to use, right?

00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:54.600
You can specify which
configs you want in there.

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:55.460
You can also do targeting.

00:32:55.460 --> 00:32:57.650
So you can say that if there's
a policy that you want that only

00:32:57.650 --> 00:32:59.455
applies to a part
of the organization,

00:32:59.455 --> 00:33:00.830
you can actually
limit that down.

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:02.558
So this policy, we
have it targeting

00:33:02.558 --> 00:33:04.100
the entire organization,
or actually,

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:05.225
in fact, all organizations.

00:33:05.225 --> 00:33:06.975
But that could just
as easily be targeting

00:33:06.975 --> 00:33:08.640
a specific folder or
a specific project

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:09.640
within the organization.

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:11.583
So you have that
flexibility to scope things

00:33:11.583 --> 00:33:14.000
down if there's particular
resources that are more secure,

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:16.130
certain environments that have
more sense of data in them.

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:18.130
You can have more restrictive
policies on those,

00:33:18.130 --> 00:33:19.970
versus a sandbox
environment where you might

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:21.630
have less restrictive policies.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:23.378
Similarly, you can
have the exclusions.

00:33:23.378 --> 00:33:24.920
By default, there's
nothing excluded,

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:28.670
but if you have, for example, a
piece of legacy infrastructure

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.920
that needs to run with an
external IP for some reason,

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:33.500
you can actually scope that down
and put an exclusion in there

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:35.880
for just that project where
that infrastructure is running.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.255
So you could actually have
whitelisting on those policies

00:33:38.255 --> 00:33:38.790
as well.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:40.400
So really flexible.

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:42.170
In all of this code,
it's really nice.

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:43.670
You can just check
this into a repo.

00:33:43.670 --> 00:33:45.503
Your policies, you work
on them the same way

00:33:45.503 --> 00:33:48.480
you work on your config as well.

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:49.800
You can also do custom logic.

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:51.350
So this is an example
of if you want

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:53.210
to do-- this is the Rego code.

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:55.730
Rego is an open source
policy language,

00:33:55.730 --> 00:33:57.680
that you can actually
take that Rego language,

00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.360
and you can write your
own code policies in it.

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:01.900
So you can say, I want to have
a custom constraint that's

00:34:01.900 --> 00:34:05.800
doing something like checking
that my labels actually

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:08.105
match the format for my
naming convention, right?

00:34:08.105 --> 00:34:09.980
So you may have an
internal naming convention

00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:11.943
that specifies that
every project has

00:34:11.943 --> 00:34:13.610
to match a certain
standard, has to have

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:14.940
costs that are embedded in it.

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:16.580
You can just write
some code that

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:19.510
enforces that constraint, embed
it into the policy library,

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:22.010
and then all of your Terraform
code that's getting submitted

00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:24.737
and all of your ongoing
module with Forseti

00:34:24.737 --> 00:34:26.570
would automatically
pick up that constraint.

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:28.909
So you have full flexibility
to define your own policies.

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:31.076
You're not constrained by
what Google has available.

00:34:31.076 --> 00:34:34.009
This is just an example
of some of that logic.

00:34:34.009 --> 00:34:36.259
So now let's look at what
that looks like in practice.

00:34:39.404 --> 00:34:41.800
Cool.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:45.043
So we showed this foundational
infrastructure before.

00:34:45.043 --> 00:34:46.710
Now I'm going to put
on my developer hat

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:49.710
and start to deploy a VM.

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:53.139
So this app that I'm working on
is only in the dev environment

00:34:53.139 --> 00:34:53.639
so far.

00:34:53.639 --> 00:34:55.320
I haven't promoted
it to production.

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:59.430
But I just have an empty
main.tf file for it.

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:01.650
So I'm going to go
and edit this file.

00:35:01.650 --> 00:35:05.353
I've got my handy dandy VM here.

00:35:05.353 --> 00:35:07.020
So there's a couple
of things I'm doing.

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:09.150
I'm just going to create
a simple compute instance

00:35:09.150 --> 00:35:10.692
for my backend
service that I'm going

00:35:10.692 --> 00:35:12.360
to be deploying
into GCP, specifying

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:14.655
the region, the
project name on it.

00:35:14.655 --> 00:35:17.280
One of the really cool things I
can do with having my Terraform

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.160
state stored in
Google Cloud Storage

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:21.240
is I can do references
across deployment,

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:24.533
so that even though I'm not
editing the networking state,

00:35:24.533 --> 00:35:25.950
I do actually have
to reference it

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:28.230
because I have to know what
subnet to put my VM on.

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:29.772
So I can actually
very easily do that

00:35:29.772 --> 00:35:32.160
by referencing this
Terraform remote state, which

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:34.003
goes and pulls from
the GCS bucket,

00:35:34.003 --> 00:35:35.670
gets the latest
networking config that's

00:35:35.670 --> 00:35:36.990
been deployed into dev.

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:40.410
It actually grabs what
the app subnet ID is.

00:35:40.410 --> 00:35:43.295
So whatever expert the
networking team has identified

00:35:43.295 --> 00:35:44.670
as this is the
subnet we want you

00:35:44.670 --> 00:35:46.795
for your applications on
there, all they have to do

00:35:46.795 --> 00:35:47.528
is export that.

00:35:47.528 --> 00:35:50.070
And I can just import that very
easily in my Terraform config

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:51.930
without ever having even
made a configuration there.

00:35:51.930 --> 00:35:53.700
So this means the next
time for some reason,

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:56.130
I have to change the subnet,
they can easily update that,

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:58.230
and my config automatically
grabs the latest one.

00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:01.020
I don't have to worry about
making any changes myself.

00:36:01.020 --> 00:36:02.760
So this looks cool.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:03.690
I'm going to do that.

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:06.107
But this time, I'm not going
to commit directly to master.

00:36:06.107 --> 00:36:08.640
I'm going to be a
good safe developer

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:10.260
and start a feature branch.

00:36:15.386 --> 00:36:16.330
Cool.

00:36:16.330 --> 00:36:20.597
So I'm going to create
a feature branch.

00:36:20.597 --> 00:36:22.180
Let's put in a nice
pull request name.

00:36:28.012 --> 00:36:28.990
Cool.

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:32.230
And this immediately
kicks off of Cloud Build.

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:34.540
Now unlike our
previous Cloud Builds,

00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:36.820
this one is actually
just doing testing.

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:38.330
It's not deploying
anything, right?

00:36:38.330 --> 00:36:39.788
So the first thing
it does, it just

00:36:39.788 --> 00:36:41.860
runs Terraform init as before.

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:43.840
But it actually
does Terraform plan.

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:45.880
So I can go in here and
look at a plan of what's

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:46.850
expected to be created.

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:50.110
So I can see it's expecting that
it's going to create a Google

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:51.650
Compute instance.

00:36:51.650 --> 00:36:54.460
It's going to have these
parameters, what IP forward.

00:36:54.460 --> 00:36:57.158
It's going to be
on this network.

00:36:57.158 --> 00:36:58.200
You can see which subnet.

00:36:58.200 --> 00:37:00.823
So the actual subnet gets
filled in and completed there.

00:37:00.823 --> 00:37:02.990
So earlier in my code, I
just have it as a variable.

00:37:02.990 --> 00:37:07.010
But here I can see, oh, this is
the us-west1 subnet dev subnet

00:37:07.010 --> 00:37:07.510
01.

00:37:07.510 --> 00:37:10.010
So I can make sure that-- do a
sanity check there, make sure

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:11.753
everything looks good to me.

00:37:11.753 --> 00:37:14.170
And then finally, it actually
passes it into the Terraform

00:37:14.170 --> 00:37:15.730
Validator, right?

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:18.080
And uh oh, it found violations.

00:37:18.080 --> 00:37:22.240
Constraint forbid
external IP on resource--

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:25.360
resource my frontend instance,
no external IPs are allowed.

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:27.830
So I accidentally put an
external IP on my instance,

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:30.820
even though my organization has
a policy of no external IPs.

00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:32.740
Now if this config had
been merged earlier,

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:34.780
I would've accidentally exposed
my network to the internet.

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:37.238
And we could have easily had
a big issue where there's IPs,

00:37:37.238 --> 00:37:39.155
I might not have had the
right firewall rules,

00:37:39.155 --> 00:37:41.500
I might not have the right
security software on that VM,

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:43.668
and I might be getting
a call from security.

00:37:43.668 --> 00:37:45.460
Thankfully, this got
caught before anything

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:46.630
was actually deployed.

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:49.300
And I had the full ability
to know about that.

00:37:49.300 --> 00:37:50.860
In fact, the check
failed, right?

00:37:50.860 --> 00:37:52.360
So GitHub is not
even going to allow

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:55.390
me to merge that pull request,
except if you're administrator.

00:37:55.390 --> 00:37:58.080
Because the check failed because
my policy was violated there,

00:37:58.080 --> 00:37:58.580
right?

00:37:58.580 --> 00:37:59.670
And I can go back
to the Cloud Build,

00:37:59.670 --> 00:38:00.837
look at the details on that.

00:38:00.837 --> 00:38:04.030
But having this enforcement of
the policy compliance at the

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:05.810
build stage with the
CI checks that we

00:38:05.810 --> 00:38:07.040
were talking about earlier.

00:38:07.040 --> 00:38:11.620
Now let's go ahead
and update that again.

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:13.870
Because we want to make
sure that we actually

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:16.630
can deploy because we want
to have a little bit of--

00:38:16.630 --> 00:38:19.160
we want to make sure that we
can take off the external IP.

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:22.120
So if I go here,
go to the main.tf

00:38:22.120 --> 00:38:25.360
that I have on my
branch, and we're just

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:27.268
going to take off this
access config part.

00:38:27.268 --> 00:38:28.810
Because the access
config is actually

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:31.880
what gives it an external IP.

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:35.461
So I can make those changes.

00:38:35.461 --> 00:38:37.060
I go back to my pull requests.

00:38:37.060 --> 00:38:38.870
It's going to rerun
the CI on it because I

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:41.110
updated my main.tf again.

00:38:41.110 --> 00:38:43.030
It's going to do a
new Terraform plan,

00:38:43.030 --> 00:38:45.162
so I can make sure
that whatever change

00:38:45.162 --> 00:38:46.870
I merge is the latest
Terraform plan so I

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:48.162
have full visibility into that.

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:59.080
And yep, just have
to pull these down.

00:38:59.080 --> 00:39:02.280
And you notice how the fact
that access config by itself

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:03.870
automatically gets
an external IP

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:05.647
isn't actually always
entirely intuitive.

00:39:05.647 --> 00:39:07.230
So that's one of the
benefits of this,

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:08.820
is that even if somebody
does something like just copy

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:11.730
and paste an example from the
internet that looks correct,

00:39:11.730 --> 00:39:15.040
looks intuitive, make sure you
can detect those violations,

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:16.710
and you can detect
misconfigurations

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:18.762
before they're applied.

00:39:18.762 --> 00:39:19.650
Cool.

00:39:19.650 --> 00:39:21.180
So cool.

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:23.610
I see my Terraform plan,
and no violations found.

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:25.470
Because I don't have
an external IP now.

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:27.630
If we go up here and look
at it, we see, oh, cool,

00:39:27.630 --> 00:39:29.813
there's no external IP on this.

00:39:29.813 --> 00:39:31.980
There's no access config
for this network interface,

00:39:31.980 --> 00:39:34.015
and there's no external
IP associated with that.

00:39:34.015 --> 00:39:35.640
So I'm just going to
go ahead and merge

00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:38.040
that because I can merge
now because there's nothing

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:38.640
blocking it.

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:41.880
All my policies passed.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:45.510
And that itself will also
kick off another CI run

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:46.880
to deploy my app to dev.

00:39:46.880 --> 00:39:49.330
So I can actually automatically
have my instance deployed,

00:39:49.330 --> 00:39:52.120
doing everything just through
GitHub and through CI workflow.

00:39:56.840 --> 00:39:59.830
And in a minute, it'll actually
deploy the instance up to here.

00:40:06.662 --> 00:40:08.490
Yep, there we go.

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:11.420
My front end instance
is being created.

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:17.280
Can go back to the slides.

00:40:17.280 --> 00:40:17.840
Cool.

00:40:17.840 --> 00:40:19.970
So that's the demos
we have today.

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:22.490
The key takeaways-- check out
the Cloud Furnishing Toolkit

00:40:22.490 --> 00:40:24.282
to get the templates
for Deployment Manager

00:40:24.282 --> 00:40:25.000
and Terraform.

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:26.750
So you can get
started really easily.

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:28.125
Check out the
Terraform Validator

00:40:28.125 --> 00:40:30.800
to make sure that you
have more reliability

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:33.280
in your infrastructure
as code pipeline

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:35.930
so that you can detect policy
violations before they actually

00:40:35.930 --> 00:40:37.040
happen.

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:39.423
And make sure you can
have security by actually

00:40:39.423 --> 00:40:40.590
doing Cloud Build pipelines.

00:40:40.590 --> 00:40:42.590
So you don't have to do
any management yourself.

00:40:42.590 --> 00:40:45.060
Nobody's logging
into any consoles.

00:40:45.060 --> 00:40:47.360
I never touched a shell
in this entire demo.

00:40:47.360 --> 00:40:49.370
I was just working
entirely on GitHub.

00:40:49.370 --> 00:40:51.453
So you can do collaborating
on your infrastructure

00:40:51.453 --> 00:40:53.780
entirely through code, note
never touching a shell.

00:40:53.780 --> 00:40:55.322
And use of the Cloud
Build pipelines,

00:40:55.322 --> 00:40:57.560
so we have example
at these links.

00:40:57.560 --> 00:41:00.272
And I think we have a Dory open
for questions if you have any,

00:41:00.272 --> 00:41:01.730
as well as we have
mics around here

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:04.627
if you want to walk up
and ask questions, too.

